From e93b02dda1f9d70440734d3c787768d2074c3fad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicolas Anastassacos <32364276+nanastassacos@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 23:28:20 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] metalscope: per-request capture and steering for Qwen3.8 and
 DeepSeek V4.1 on Metal

Everything here is off unless ds4-server is started with --scope-dir DIR: without it a request's
"scope" object is a key the parsers skip, a completion's prompt of numbers is read as before, and
there is no tokenize endpoint.

With it, a request's "scope" object (chat, completions, messages, responses) makes ds4 write a binary
capture file (format 6: prompt, per-step routing, stream norms, what each layer changed in each
stream, logit lens, top-k, log-probabilities at given ids, the J-lens, rewinds, inserted text, and per-layer
residual sums over the prompt) and steer: per layer, up to 8 orthonormal directions projected out of
the residual row (f32 sums), then a vector added to it (steering file version 2). Completions take a
token-id prompt fed exactly (no template, no text-keyed prefix reuse); POST /v1/scope/tokenize
returns ds4's own tokens for a text or a chat. Scoped sessions skip native session batching and the
cold-store prefix sync.
A scoped request reuses the rows the live session holds, as any request does, when they were computed
under the same steering (the session keeps a signature of its rows' steering; a request without a
scope empties a steered session first), and records only the rows it feeds. Positions=decode
steering, residual sums and "fresh" read the whole prompt. Scoped requests and steered rows stay out
of the disk KV cache. A traced request's "next_lp" reads each prompt token's log-probability
(NXLP) and the row's top 10 next tokens (NXTK): on Qwen3.8 through the head in tiles, on V4.1 (its
sweep reads no row's head) by feeding those rows one at a time. "lookup": false leaves the n-gram
lookup (Qwen3.8's PLE, V4.1's Engram) out of its forwards, the session's rows dropped before and
after: metalscope's lookup measurement.
The files a scope names must resolve inside DIR, and the trace is created new (a name that exists is
refused). A trace that stops partway says why after END_'s finish; one whose write failed has no END_.

The code lives in its own files, included as text where it hooks in: ds4_scope.inc (capture),
ds4_scope_ds41.inc and ds4_scope_qwen4.inc (graph hooks), ds4_scope_session.inc (session API,
ds4_scope.h), ds4_metal_scope.inc and metal/scope.metal (kernels, ds4_gpu_scope.h),
ds4_server_scope.inc and ds4_server_scope_job.inc (server), tests/ds4_scope_test.inc.

The new files (ds4_scope.h, ds4_scope*.inc, ds4_gpu_scope.h, ds4_metal_scope.inc, ds4_server_scope*.inc,
metal/scope.metal, tests/ds4_scope_test.inc) are contributed under ds4's MIT licence (LICENSE).
